  - All subnets must have disjoint address ranges.
  - Every system must belong to the address range of a subnet.
  - There must be exactly one router per subnet.
  - Adhere to all Checkstyle rules; some marked rules are mandatory and cause zero points if
  violated.
  - Adhere to the prescribed whitespace rules.
  - After start the program must read commands from standard input, execute them on current state,
  and wait for further inputs (i.e., keep running interactively).
  - Choose appropriate visibility modifiers for classes, methods, and attributes.
  - Choose meaningful identifier names for all identifiers.
  - Comments should be consistently written either in English or German.
  - Do not add personal data other than your u-Kürzel to submissions.
  - Do not call System.exit(), Runtime.exit(), or similar methods.
  - Do not place classes in the default (unnamed) package.
  - Enforce a maximum line width of 140 characters in source files.
  - Follow the Javadoc documentation rules specified by the assignment.
  - Follow the rules for variable, method, and package naming.
  - In the Javadoc @author tag include only your u-Kürzel.
  - Interactive user interface outputs must match the specification exactly (including case, spaces,
   and newlines); do not print additional information.
  - Mandatory tests must be passed before other tests are evaluated; violation causes automatic zero
   points for the submission.
  - Other Checkstyle rule violations may lead to point deductions (but not automatic zero).
  - Program code must compile without errors.
  - Program source code must be written in English.
  - Submissions are evaluated on both object-oriented modelling and functionality; follow the
  modelling guidance in the ILIAS wiki.
  - Submit only the required *.java files for task A as individual work with the corresponding
  folder structure.
  - The online submission system checks source files with Checkstyle automatically during
  submission.
  - Unless explicitly allowed, do not use other Java library elements; allowed exceptions are
  java.util.Scanner and elements from the listed packages.
  - Use exclusively Java SE 17 for implementation.
  - Use System.in for input and System.out for output unless otherwise specified; System.err may be
  used for error messages; never reassign standard streams.
  - After printing an error message the program must continue and wait for the next input.
  - Commands must not violate given semantic and syntactic specifications; on violation an
  informative error must be shown.
  - If user input does not match the required format, the program must output an error message.
  - All edges within a subnet must be weighted.
  - An error message must not contain special characters such as newlines or umlauts.
  - Error messages may be freely chosen in English but must be meaningful (explain why input was
  rejected).
  - Error messages should indicate why the input was rejected (be explanatory).
  - Every error message must begin with the literal prefix 'Error,'.
  </adaptive_rules>

